> 
> ## 6. Failures that compile, run, and are still wrong
> 
> These do not fail the build. Nothing warns you. That is the only reason they are written down.
> 
> ### A plain field reference IS a trigger
> 
> Holding another node in a field makes it a **trigger parent**: any change to it fires your `@OnTrigger`.
> That is the surprising default exactly when you meant "read this, don't react to it" — a lookup table, a
> price book, a config holder.
> 
> ```java
> public class ReorderPolicy {
>     private final StockLedger ledger;
>     private final SkuCatalog catalog;   // intended as a lookup only
>     @OnTrigger public boolean recompute() { ... }
> }
> ```
> 
> compiles to `guardCheck_reorderPolicy() { return isDirty_skuCatalog | isDirty_stockLedger; }` — so every
> catalog change now recomputes the policy.
> 
> **Fix:** `@NoTriggerReference` (FQN `com.telamin.fluxtion.runtime.annotations.NoTriggerReference` — note
> this is NOT in `annotations.builder`, unlike `@FluxtionIgnore`) on any reference you only read from.
> 
> ### One `@OnTrigger` per node, not one per parent
> 
> `@OnTrigger` is already an OR over **all** parent dirty flags, so a node with five parents needs **one**
> method, not five. Writing one per parent does not give per-parent dispatch — every method fires on every
> change, and each **overwrites the node's dirty flag**, so only the last return propagates.
> 
> **Fix:** one `@OnTrigger`. If you must know which parent changed, that is `@OnParentUpdate`, which takes
> the parent's type as its single argument.
